[
  {
    "topicTitle": "Testing Fundamentals",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-1",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — Purpose and Role of Software Testing",
        "content": "Software testing is the disciplined process of evaluating an application to determine whether its behavior matches documented requirements and real user expectations. Testing is not limited to finding defects after development. It provides continuous evidence that individual components, integrated services, business workflows, and release artifacts behave correctly under defined conditions. In a Maven and Spring Boot delivery pipeline, tests can execute automatically whenever code is committed or a pull request is opened. A useful test describes the expected behavior, supplies controlled input, executes the relevant application logic, and verifies the observed result. Testing reduces the risk of delivering regressions, incorrect calculations, broken integrations, security weaknesses, and operational failures. It cannot prove that software has no defects; it increases confidence by examining selected scenarios systematically. Teams should connect every important test to a requirement, business rule, or known failure mode so that the test suite remains meaningful rather than becoming a collection of arbitrary assertions."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-2",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — Verification, Validation, and Quality Objectives",
        "content": "Verification and validation are related but different quality activities. Verification asks whether the software was built according to its specifications, design, and implementation rules. Examples include checking method behavior against an interface contract, validating configuration structure, and confirming that a component follows an agreed technical design. Validation asks whether the delivered system solves the actual user or business problem. A service can pass every narrow unit test and still fail validation if its workflow does not meet customer expectations. A practical test strategy combines both perspectives. Verification tests should cover defined rules, boundary conditions, error handling, and technical contracts. Validation should exercise realistic user journeys and business outcomes. Quality objectives should be measurable: expected results, acceptable response behavior, supported input ranges, error conditions, and release-blocking failures must be explicit. In CI, these objectives become automated checks that provide repeatable evidence before a change is accepted or deployed."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-3",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — Test Levels and Their Responsibilities",
        "content": "Software tests are commonly organized into levels according to the scope of behavior being evaluated. Unit tests examine a small unit such as a method or class with collaborators controlled where appropriate. Component tests validate a larger application component and its internal interactions. Integration tests verify communication across boundaries such as application code and a database, message broker, filesystem, or external service. System tests evaluate the assembled application as a whole, while acceptance tests assess whether business workflows satisfy agreed acceptance criteria. These levels answer different questions and should not be treated as interchangeable. A unit test that mocks a repository cannot prove that the real database schema is compatible. An integration test that starts a database does not automatically prove that every user workflow is correct. Assign each test to the level that can provide reliable evidence with the least unnecessary complexity, and make the level visible through naming, test configuration, and CI execution."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-4",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — The Test Pyramid and Feedback Speed",
        "content": "The test pyramid is a planning model that encourages a broad base of fast, focused tests and a smaller number of slower, broader tests. Unit tests usually form the base because they execute quickly and isolate defects close to the code that caused them. Integration tests occupy the middle because they validate real component boundaries and require additional infrastructure or setup. End-to-end and acceptance tests form a smaller upper layer because they exercise complete workflows and are often more expensive to execute and maintain. The pyramid is guidance rather than a mandatory numerical ratio. A service with complex database behavior may need substantial integration coverage, while a user interface may require more browser-level checks. The important principle is to obtain fast feedback early and reserve expensive tests for risks that narrower tests cannot evaluate. If a pipeline relies almost entirely on slow end-to-end tests, developers may wait too long to identify simple logic defects."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-5",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — Test Cases, Inputs, Expected Results, and Boundaries",
        "content": "A test case should define the condition being examined, the input or initial state, the action performed, and the expected observable result. Good test cases cover normal behavior as well as invalid input, missing values, empty collections, minimum and maximum boundaries, and relevant combinations of conditions. For example, a service that calculates a discount should be tested with an eligible customer, an ineligible customer, a zero-value order, a threshold boundary, and an invalid amount. Expected results should be explicit rather than calculated using the same production logic being tested, because repeating the implementation can reproduce the same defect in the test. Test names should describe behavior and condition so failures are understandable in CI reports. A test suite should also avoid redundant cases that exercise identical behavior without adding confidence. Review test cases whenever requirements or business rules change, and retain regression cases for defects that previously escaped into production."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-6",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — Test Isolation, Independence, and Repeatability",
        "content": "A reliable test should produce the same result when it runs alone, in a different order, or as part of the complete suite. Test isolation prevents one test from depending on data, static state, environment variables, or execution side effects left by another test. Each test should establish the state it needs and clean up resources it creates. Shared databases require controlled records, unique identifiers, transaction strategies, or dedicated schemas so concurrent runs do not interfere with one another. Tests should not depend on a developer-specific directory, an undocumented local service, or a manually prepared database. Repeatability also requires controlling time, randomness, network responses, and external dependencies where those factors are not the subject of the test. In CI, parallel execution can expose hidden shared-state assumptions. When a test passes locally but fails intermittently on a build agent, investigate isolation and environmental dependencies before simply rerunning the pipeline."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-7",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — Test Data and Environment Management",
        "content": "Test data is part of the test design and must be predictable, representative, and safe to use. A test should create the minimum data needed for its scenario, use clear identifiers, and avoid relying on records that happen to exist in a shared environment. Synthetic data is preferable when real customer information is unnecessary because it reduces privacy and compliance risks. Environment configuration should be explicit: database connection details, active Spring profiles, service endpoints, credentials, and feature flags must be supplied through controlled test configuration rather than hard-coded secrets. Integration tests may use disposable databases or containers to make infrastructure behavior reproducible. Test setup should fail clearly when a required dependency is unavailable instead of silently skipping important checks. Cleanup must remove temporary resources even when an assertion fails. These practices help developers reproduce failures locally and allow CI agents to execute the same test scenarios consistently."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-8",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — Defect Detection, Regression, and Risk-Based Testing",
        "content": "A defect is a mismatch between actual and expected behavior. When a defect is discovered, the team should identify the affected requirement, determine the conditions that trigger the problem, and create a regression test that fails before the correction and passes after it. Regression testing protects previously working behavior when code, dependencies, configuration, or infrastructure changes. Risk-based testing helps prioritize effort by considering the likelihood of failure and its potential impact. Authentication, authorization, payment calculations, data persistence, migration logic, and release-critical workflows generally deserve stronger coverage than low-impact cosmetic behavior. A test suite should not pursue coverage numbers without considering business risk. A high percentage of executed lines does not guarantee that important outcomes or failure paths are checked. Combine requirement-based cases, defect history, code changes, and production incident patterns to decide where additional tests provide the greatest reduction in release risk."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-9",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — Assertions and Meaningful Failure Messages",
        "content": "Assertions compare actual behavior with an expected condition and determine whether a test passes or fails. Useful assertions verify outcomes that matter to the behavior under test, such as returned values, state changes, emitted events, persisted records, or expected exceptions. A test that only checks that a method did not throw an exception may provide insufficient evidence when the method is expected to update important business state. Assertions should be specific enough to explain what was wrong. Prefer checking meaningful fields and conditions over comparing unrelated implementation details that change frequently. Exception tests should verify the expected exception type and, when relevant, important message or error details. Avoid excessive assertions that combine many unrelated behaviors into one test, because a single failure can obscure the actual problem. Clear assertion messages and descriptive test names make Maven Surefire and Failsafe reports easier to diagnose during automated builds."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-10",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — Test Automation in a Maven CI Pipeline",
        "content": "Automated tests become part of the delivery process when the build system executes them consistently and reports their results. In a Maven project, the test phase commonly runs unit tests through the Maven Surefire Plugin, while integration tests can be bound to the integration-test and verify phases through the Maven Failsafe Plugin. A typical CI job checks out the source, selects the required JDK, resolves dependencies, compiles the project, executes tests, packages the application, and archives reports and artifacts. A failed test should normally produce a non-zero build result so that a broken change cannot pass silently. Teams should retain test reports even when a job fails, because the reports provide evidence for diagnosis. Build agents should use controlled tool versions and configuration. Do not hide failures with options that ignore test errors in a release pipeline. Separate fast pull-request feedback from broader scheduled or release validation when execution cost requires it."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-11",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — Coverage Metrics and Their Limitations",
        "content": "Code coverage measures which portions of source code were executed while tests ran. Common measures include line coverage, instruction coverage, branch coverage, and method coverage. These metrics can reveal unexecuted areas and help teams identify code that may need additional tests. However, execution is not the same as verification: a test can execute a line without asserting that its result is correct. Branch coverage can show whether alternative decision paths were exercised, but it does not prove that every meaningful input combination has been tested. Coverage thresholds are most useful when applied consistently and interpreted alongside risk, test quality, and defect trends. JaCoCo is commonly used in Maven projects to collect coverage data and generate reports. Teams should examine uncovered critical logic, error handling, and boundary conditions rather than trying to increase a percentage through meaningless tests. Coverage should support engineering judgment, not replace it."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-1-section-12",
        "title": "Testing Fundamentals — Designing a Sustainable Test Strategy",
        "content": "A sustainable test strategy defines what must be tested, at which level, with which tools, and at what point in the delivery lifecycle. Start by identifying business-critical workflows, external dependencies, data boundaries, operational risks, and failure history. Map these risks to unit, component, integration, system, and acceptance tests. Define ownership for test data, environment setup, test maintenance, and failure investigation. Establish clear rules for pull-request checks, main-branch validation, scheduled regression suites, and release gates. Keep fast feedback close to development while ensuring that broader tests execute before production release. Review flaky tests, obsolete scenarios, slow setup, and duplicate coverage regularly. A test that fails intermittently should be investigated and corrected rather than permanently ignored. The strategy should evolve as the application architecture changes, new services are introduced, and production incidents reveal missing coverage. Its goal is dependable release evidence, not simply a large number of test files."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "JUnit 5 Architecture",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-1",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Testing Strategy and Fundamentals",
        "content": "# JUnit 5 Architecture — Testing Strategy and Fundamentals\n\n## Learning Objective\nJUnit 5 is the modern Java testing platform used to write, discover, and execute automated tests. It is not a single library. JUnit 5 consists of JUnit Platform, JUnit Jupiter, and JUnit Vintage. Understanding these parts helps developers configure test execution correctly in Maven, Gradle, IDEs, and CI pipelines.\n\n## Core Architecture\nJUnit Platform provides the foundation for launching testing frameworks. JUnit Jupiter provides the programming model and extension model for writing JUnit 5 tests. JUnit Vintage allows older JUnit 3 and JUnit 4 tests to run on the JUnit Platform during migration.\n\n## Practical Example\nA basic Jupiter test uses `@Test` from `org.junit.jupiter.api.Test`:\n\n```java\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;\nimport static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;\n\nclass CalculatorTest {\n    @Test\n    void addsTwoNumbers() {\n        Calculator calculator = new Calculator();\n        assertEquals(5, calculator.add(2, 3));\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe test verifies observable behavior rather than implementation details. If the result differs from the expected value, JUnit reports a failure with the expected and actual values.\n\n## DevOps Application\nA CI pipeline runs the test suite before packaging an application. A failing JUnit test should fail the Maven or Gradle test task and prevent an invalid build artifact from being promoted.\n\n## Key Takeaway\nUse Jupiter for new tests, Platform-compatible launchers for execution, and Vintage only when legacy JUnit tests still need to run."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-2",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Framework Architecture",
        "content": "# JUnit 5 Architecture — Framework Architecture\n\n## Learning Objective\nUnderstand the three major JUnit 5 components and how they cooperate during test execution.\n\n## Platform, Jupiter, and Vintage\nJUnit Platform is the launcher and test-engine infrastructure. It discovers test engines, selects tests, executes them, and publishes execution results. JUnit Jupiter is the engine and API for JUnit 5 tests, including annotations such as `@Test`, `@BeforeEach`, and `@ParameterizedTest`. JUnit Vintage is an optional engine for running JUnit 3 and JUnit 4 tests.\n\n## Execution Flow\nA build tool or IDE starts the JUnit Platform launcher. The launcher discovers available test engines. The Jupiter engine discovers Jupiter test classes and methods, creates the required test instances, runs lifecycle callbacks, executes test methods, and reports results.\n\n## Maven Configuration\nA Maven project normally uses `junit-jupiter` as a test dependency and a compatible Surefire version to execute tests:\n\n```xml\n<dependency>\n  <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>\n  <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>\n  <version>5.10.2</version>\n  <scope>test</scope>\n</dependency>\n```\n\nKeep dependency and plugin versions controlled through the project build configuration.\n\n## Troubleshooting\nIf tests compile but are not discovered, verify the test engine dependency, Surefire version, test class naming, and package structure. Check `mvn test` output rather than assuming that a successful compilation means tests executed.\n\n## Key Takeaway\nThe Platform launches tests, Jupiter supplies the modern testing model, and Vintage supports legacy test migration."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-3",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Test Design and Implementation",
        "content": "# JUnit 5 Architecture — Test Design and Implementation\n\n## Learning Objective\nCreate tests that verify business behavior, remain understandable, and detect regressions without depending on internal implementation details.\n\n## Test Structure\nA well-designed test has three logical steps: arrange the required inputs and dependencies, act by invoking the behavior under test, and assert the observable result. Keep each test focused on one behavior or one clearly related set of outcomes.\n\n## Example\n```java\n@Test\nvoid rejectsNegativeQuantity() {\n    OrderService service = new OrderService();\n    IllegalArgumentException error = assertThrows(\n        IllegalArgumentException.class,\n        () -> service.calculateTotal(-2, 100)\n    );\n    assertEquals(\"Quantity must be positive\", error.getMessage());\n}\n```\n\nThe test checks both the exception type and its meaningful message. It does not inspect private fields or rely on the order of internal method calls.\n\n## Design Guidance\nUse descriptive names such as `rejectsNegativeQuantity`. Include boundary values, valid inputs, invalid inputs, and important business rules. Avoid tests that depend on current time, random values, shared mutable state, or external services unless those dependencies are controlled.\n\n## DevOps Application\nReliable tests provide fast feedback in pull requests. Small, deterministic tests make failures easier to diagnose and reduce unnecessary CI reruns.\n\n## Key Takeaway\nTest externally visible behavior and make failures explain exactly which business rule was violated."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-4",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Assertions and Lifecycle",
        "content": "# JUnit 5 Architecture — Assertions and Lifecycle\n\n## Learning Objective\nUse JUnit assertions and lifecycle callbacks to control test setup, execution, and cleanup.\n\n## Assertions\nJUnit Jupiter provides assertions including `assertEquals`, `assertTrue`, `assertFalse`, `assertNull`, `assertNotNull`, `assertThrows`, and `assertAll`. Assertions should express the expected behavior clearly. `assertAll` is useful when several independent properties should be checked together.\n\n## Lifecycle Annotations\n`@BeforeEach` runs before each test method. `@AfterEach` runs after each test method. `@BeforeAll` and `@AfterAll` run once for the test class; by default, these methods must be static unless the test class uses `@TestInstance(TestInstance.Lifecycle.PER_CLASS)`.\n\n## Example\n```java\nclass AccountTest {\n    private Account account;\n\n    @BeforeEach\n    void setUp() {\n        account = new Account(100);\n    }\n\n    @Test\n    void withdrawReducesBalance() {\n        account.withdraw(30);\n        assertEquals(70, account.getBalance());\n    }\n\n    @AfterEach\n    void cleanUp() {\n        account = null;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Common Mistakes\nDo not put test-specific state in static fields without a clear reason. Avoid relying on test execution order. Each test should be independently executable.\n\n## Key Takeaway\nUse lifecycle callbacks for predictable setup and cleanup, and assertions for clear verification of outcomes."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-5",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Mocking and Dependency Isolation",
        "content": "# JUnit 5 Architecture — Mocking and Dependency Isolation\n\n## Learning Objective\nUnderstand why unit tests isolate external dependencies and how JUnit 5 works with mocking libraries such as Mockito.\n\n## Dependency Isolation\nA unit test should normally exercise one unit of application behavior without requiring a real database, network service, message broker, or filesystem. Mockito can create mock collaborators whose responses are controlled by the test.\n\n## Example with Mockito\n```java\n@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)\nclass PaymentServiceTest {\n    @Mock PaymentGateway gateway;\n    @InjectMocks PaymentService service;\n\n    @Test\n    void returnsApprovedWhenGatewayApproves() {\n        when(gateway.charge(500)).thenReturn(true);\n\n        boolean result = service.pay(500);\n\n        assertTrue(result);\n        verify(gateway).charge(500);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe Mockito extension initializes annotated mocks for each test context. The test configures the gateway response and verifies the service result and required interaction.\n\n## Design Guidance\nMock boundaries such as remote clients and repositories, not every object in the application. Excessive mocking can make tests verify implementation details instead of behavior. Use real simple domain objects where practical.\n\n## Troubleshooting\nIf a mock is unexpectedly null, check extension registration, annotation usage, and test initialization. If a stub is unused, verify that the code path actually calls the mocked method.\n\n## Key Takeaway\nJUnit provides test execution and assertions; Mockito provides controlled test doubles for dependencies."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-6",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Unit and Integration Testing",
        "content": "# JUnit 5 Architecture — Unit and Integration Testing\n\n## Learning Objective\nDistinguish unit tests from integration tests and select the correct level for each requirement.\n\n## Unit Tests\nA unit test checks a small component in isolation. It is usually fast and uses controlled collaborators. Examples include validating a calculation, checking input validation, or testing a business rule.\n\n## Integration Tests\nAn integration test verifies that multiple components work together. It may involve Spring application configuration, persistence, serialization, HTTP endpoints, or a real database instance. Integration tests are slower because they initialize more infrastructure.\n\n## Example\nA unit test for a service can mock its repository. An integration test can start a Spring application context and verify that a repository query correctly persists and retrieves an entity.\n\n## Test Selection\nUse unit tests for detailed business-rule coverage. Use integration tests for framework wiring, database mappings, transaction behavior, and communication between components. Do not use a large integration test for every small calculation.\n\n## CI Pipeline\nRun fast unit tests early in pull-request validation. Run integration tests in a controlled environment with required services and collect their reports as build artifacts.\n\n## Key Takeaway\nUnit tests isolate behavior; integration tests prove that collaborating components work together."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-7",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Container-Based Testing",
        "content": "# JUnit 5 Architecture — Container-Based Testing\n\n## Learning Objective\nUnderstand how JUnit tests can use containerized dependencies to verify application behavior against realistic infrastructure.\n\n## Why Container-Based Tests\nMocks cannot fully reproduce database SQL behavior, broker protocols, or service-specific configuration. Testcontainers can start disposable Docker containers for dependencies such as PostgreSQL, MySQL, Redis, and Kafka during automated tests.\n\n## Example\nA Java test can declare a PostgreSQL container and use its connection details to configure a data source. The test then exercises repository operations against an actual PostgreSQL server rather than an in-memory substitute.\n\n```java\n@Testcontainers\nclass RepositoryIntegrationTest {\n    @Container\n    static PostgreSQLContainer<?> database =\n        new PostgreSQLContainer<>(\"postgres:16-alpine\");\n}\n```\n\nAdd the appropriate Testcontainers modules and configure the application to use the container's mapped port and credentials.\n\n## Operational Requirements\nThe test runner needs access to a Docker-compatible runtime. Pin container image versions, avoid relying on a developer-specific local database, and ensure containers are stopped after execution.\n\n## CI Application\nA CI agent must provide Docker access or a supported container runtime. Capture logs when startup fails and distinguish infrastructure startup errors from application test failures.\n\n## Key Takeaway\nContainer-based tests provide realistic dependency behavior while keeping test infrastructure disposable and repeatable."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-8",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Coverage and Quality Measurement",
        "content": "# JUnit 5 Architecture — Coverage and Quality Measurement\n\n## Learning Objective\nMeasure which parts of application code are exercised by tests and understand the limitations of coverage metrics.\n\n## Coverage Types\nLine coverage measures executed source lines. Branch coverage measures whether decision outcomes have been exercised. Instruction and method coverage provide additional views depending on the coverage tool.\n\n## JaCoCo Integration\nJaCoCo instruments Java execution and produces coverage data that can be converted into HTML and XML reports. Maven projects commonly use the JaCoCo Maven plugin to prepare the agent, run tests, and generate a report.\n\n## Quality Interpretation\nHigh coverage does not guarantee correct tests. A test may execute a line without asserting meaningful behavior. Focus on critical business branches, error handling, security checks, and boundary conditions rather than chasing a percentage alone.\n\n## CI Enforcement\nA pipeline can publish the JaCoCo report and apply a minimum coverage rule. Configure thresholds carefully and review exclusions so generated code or irrelevant packages do not distort results.\n\n## Troubleshooting\nIf the report is empty, verify that tests ran with the JaCoCo agent and that the report goal reads the execution data from the expected location.\n\n## Key Takeaway\nUse coverage to identify untested code, then assess whether tests contain meaningful assertions."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-9",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Test Reporting",
        "content": "# JUnit 5 Architecture — Test Reporting\n\n## Learning Objective\nInterpret JUnit test results and publish useful reports for developers and CI systems.\n\n## Result Categories\nA test can pass, fail because an assertion was not satisfied, or abort because execution could not continue. Build tools also report errors caused by test initialization, compilation, dependency resolution, or test-engine failures. These conditions should not be treated as identical.\n\n## Maven Reports\nMaven Surefire executes unit tests and writes XML and text reports under `target/surefire-reports`. Failsafe commonly handles integration tests and writes reports under `target/failsafe-reports`.\n\n## Investigation\nStart with the failed test name and stack trace. Identify the assertion, exception, or setup failure. Compare the failing run with the previous successful build and inspect relevant application logs.\n\n## CI Integration\nArchive test reports even when the test step fails. This allows developers to inspect failures after the pipeline has stopped. Configure the CI platform to display JUnit-compatible XML results when supported.\n\n## Key Takeaway\nA useful report identifies the exact failing test, preserves diagnostic details, and remains available after a failed pipeline."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-10",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Flaky Test Investigation",
        "content": "# JUnit 5 Architecture — Flaky Test Investigation\n\n## Learning Objective\nIdentify tests that pass and fail without a relevant code change and remove the underlying source of nondeterminism.\n\n## Common Causes\nFlaky tests often depend on timing, shared mutable state, test execution order, random input, external network services, machine load, or incomplete cleanup. Parallel execution can expose hidden shared-state problems.\n\n## Investigation Process\nRecord the failing test name, seed, environment, execution duration, and stack trace. Re-run the individual test, then run it repeatedly and with the full suite. Compare isolated and parallel execution to determine whether the failure is caused by shared resources or order dependence.\n\n## JUnit Controls\nUse `@BeforeEach` and `@AfterEach` to create and clean test state. Avoid arbitrary sleeps. Prefer waiting for a specific condition with a bounded timeout when asynchronous behavior must be tested. Use `@ResourceLock` when tests genuinely share a resource and parallel execution is enabled.\n\n## CI Response\nDo not permanently hide flaky tests by adding unlimited retries. Track failure frequency, assign ownership, and fix the source. A temporary quarantine should be visible and time-limited.\n\n## Key Takeaway\nA flaky test is a reliability defect in the test suite. Find the nondeterministic dependency instead of repeatedly rerunning the pipeline."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-11",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — CI Integration",
        "content": "# JUnit 5 Architecture — CI Integration\n\n## Learning Objective\nExecute JUnit tests consistently in continuous integration and make test failures visible to the development team.\n\n## Maven Execution\nA typical Maven validation command is:\n\n```bash\nmvn -B clean verify\n```\n\nThe `test` phase runs unit tests through Surefire. When Failsafe is configured, integration tests run during the integration-test phase and are checked during the verify phase.\n\n## Pipeline Design\nUse a clean checkout, install the required JDK, resolve dependencies, compile source code, execute tests, and publish reports. Cache Maven dependencies where appropriate, but do not reuse stale build output as a substitute for a clean validation run.\n\n## Example Jenkins Stage\n```groovy\nstage('Test') {\n    steps {\n        sh 'mvn -B clean verify'\n    }\n    post {\n        always {\n            junit testResults: '**/target/*-reports/TEST-*.xml',\n                  allowEmptyResults: true\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nAdjust report patterns to match the project's actual Surefire and Failsafe output locations.\n\n## Failure Handling\nA non-zero Maven exit code should fail the pipeline. Preserve XML reports and logs so developers can diagnose the failure. Do not mark a build successful merely because report publishing completed.\n\n## Key Takeaway\nCI must execute the real test lifecycle, fail on test failures, and retain reports for investigation."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-12",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Production Test Strategy",
        "content": "# JUnit 5 Architecture — Production Test Strategy\n\n## Learning Objective\nDesign a layered test strategy that provides fast feedback while protecting critical application behavior before release.\n\n## Test Layers\nUse unit tests for business rules and edge cases. Use integration tests for persistence, framework wiring, and service collaboration. Use API or end-to-end tests for critical user workflows. Keep the broadest tests smaller in number because they require more infrastructure and are usually slower.\n\n## Risk-Based Coverage\nPrioritize payment calculations, authorization, data validation, transaction boundaries, failure handling, and backward compatibility. Test both successful and rejected operations. Include regression tests whenever a production defect is fixed.\n\n## Reliability\nTests should be deterministic, isolated, repeatable, and safe to run concurrently where possible. External dependencies should be controlled through mocks for unit tests or disposable infrastructure for integration tests.\n\n## Release Pipeline\nRequire compilation, unit tests, integration tests, static analysis, and relevant security checks before artifact promotion. Store test reports and associate them with the exact commit and build artifact.\n\n## Production Feedback\nMonitor escaped defects and add regression tests for failures found after deployment. A test suite should evolve with incidents and changing business requirements.\n\n## Key Takeaway\nA production test strategy balances speed, realism, risk coverage, and repeatability instead of relying on one large test suite."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-13",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Hands-on Testing Exercise",
        "content": "# JUnit 5 Architecture — Hands-on Testing Exercise\n\n## Objective\nBuild and execute a small JUnit Jupiter test suite for a Java calculator service.\n\n## Implementation\nCreate a `Calculator` class with `add`, `subtract`, and `divide` methods. Define expected behavior for normal inputs and division by zero. Keep business logic separate from the test class.\n\n## Test Cases\nWrite Jupiter tests that verify positive and negative addition, subtraction, division with a non-zero divisor, and the expected exception for division by zero. Use descriptive test names and assertions such as `assertEquals` and `assertThrows`.\n\n## Execute\nRun the project tests using:\n\n```bash\nmvn test\n```\n\nInspect the Maven output and the XML reports under `target/surefire-reports`.\n\n## Failure Exercise\nTemporarily change the expected result in one test. Run the tests again and identify the failed assertion, expected value, actual value, and non-zero build status. Restore the correct expectation after observing the failure.\n\n## Completion Criteria\nAll tests pass, the test report contains the expected test cases, and the test suite can run repeatedly without depending on execution order or external infrastructure.\n\n## Key Takeaway\nA useful exercise proves both the successful path and the failure-reporting behavior of the build."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-2-section-14",
        "title": "JUnit 5 Architecture — Testing Strategy and Fundamentals — JUnit 5 Architecture [stage-5-topic-2-section-14]",
        "content": "# JUnit 5 Architecture — Migration and Compatibility\n\n## Learning Objective\nUnderstand how a Java project can migrate from JUnit 4 to JUnit Jupiter while keeping legacy tests executable during the transition.\n\n## Compatibility Model\nJUnit Vintage is the test engine for running JUnit 3 and JUnit 4 tests on the JUnit Platform. It is optional and should be retained only while the project still has legacy tests. JUnit Jupiter is the recommended programming model for new JUnit 5 tests.\n\n## Migration Considerations\nJUnit 4 uses annotations such as `org.junit.Test` and `org.junit.Before`. Jupiter uses `org.junit.jupiter.api.Test` and `org.junit.jupiter.api.BeforeEach`. Imports must be updated carefully; identical annotation names from different packages are not interchangeable.\n\n## Build Configuration\nAdd Jupiter dependencies and a compatible Surefire plugin. If legacy tests remain, include the Vintage engine as a test-scoped dependency. Remove Vintage after all legacy tests have been migrated and validated.\n\n## Validation\nRun the complete test suite before and after migration. Compare discovered test counts, failures, skipped tests, and report output. Pay special attention to lifecycle behavior, rules, runners, and parameterized tests because migration may require design changes.\n\n## Key Takeaway\nMigrate incrementally, run old and new tests through the Platform where needed, and remove compatibility components only after legacy tests are gone."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Test Annotations",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-3-section-1",
        "title": "Test Annotations — Testing Strategy and Fundamentals",
        "content": "# Test Annotations — Testing Strategy and Fundamentals\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThis section belongs to **Test Annotations** in Stage 05: Kubernetes Container Orchestration.\n\nKubernetes manages containerized applications through a declarative API and a set of controllers that continuously reconcile actual cluster state with the desired state declared in resource specifications. The relevant components may include Pods, workload controllers, Services, networking, storage, configuration resources, scheduling, security controls, and observability systems. The engineering objective is to run applications reliably, securely, and predictably across a cluster.\n\nDefine the Kubernetes concept represented by this section and explain the problem it solves. Describe where it fits in a cluster and application lifecycle. Identify the relevant API resource, controller, node component, or external integration. Explain technical terms in beginner-friendly language and distinguish Kubernetes orchestration from the responsibility of the application running inside a container.\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Kubernetes resources and components involved, perform the relevant operation, inspect cluster state, and recognize the risks of an incorrect implementation.\n\n## 2. Kubernetes Technical Architecture and Behavior\n\nKubernetes uses a declarative operating model. An engineer declares the desired state in API resources, and Kubernetes controllers continuously work to reconcile the actual cluster state with that desired state.\n\nThe API server is the main entry point for cluster operations. Authentication, authorization, and admission controls determine whether a request can be accepted. etcd stores cluster state. Controllers monitor resources and create or update dependent objects. The scheduler assigns unscheduled Pods to suitable nodes. The kubelet on each worker node works with the container runtime to ensure assigned workloads run as specified.\n\nFor **Test Annotations — Testing Strategy and Fundamentals**, identify the API resource, controller, node component, or external integration responsible for the behavior. Explain what state is submitted, what reconciliation takes place, and how the result can be observed. Do not assume that a successful kubectl apply means the application is healthy. API acceptance, scheduling, container startup, readiness, and application availability are separate conditions.\n\n## 3. Implementation Workflow\n\n1. Confirm the current Kubernetes context with kubectl config current-context.\n2. Select and verify the correct namespace.\n3. Inspect the relevant resources before changing anything.\n4. Review the manifest fields, image reference, labels, selectors, resources, and permissions.\n5. Apply the required configuration using a reviewed manifest or approved deployment process.\n6. Observe controller reconciliation and Pod scheduling.\n7. Inspect Pod conditions, container state, events, and logs.\n8. Verify networking, storage, and application readiness where required.\n9. Record the manifest revision, image digest, and resulting workload state.\n\nUse namespaces and explicit resource names to avoid changing the wrong environment. Before deleting or replacing resources, determine whether they own persistent data or production traffic.\n\n## 4. Practical Kubernetes Commands\n\nThe following commands provide relevant inspection and operational examples. Replace placeholder resource names and namespaces with actual values.\n\n`\bash id=\"k8s501\"\nkubectl cluster-info\nkubectl get nodes -o wide\nkubectl get namespaces\nkubectl get pods -A\nkubectl get services -A\nkubectl get events -A --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp\n`\n\nCommands that modify resources should be executed only after checking the active context and reviewing the target manifest. Avoid copying production credentials or secret values into terminal history.\n\n## 5. Kubernetes Workload Example\n\nThe following Deployment demonstrates a basic stateless application workload:\n\n`yaml id=\"k8s502\"\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: payments-api\n  namespace: production\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: payments-api\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: payments-api\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: application\n          image: registry.example.com/payments-api:1.4.0\n          ports:\n            - name: http\n              containerPort: 8080\n          resources:\n            requests:\n              cpu: \"250m\"\n              memory: \"256Mi\"\n            limits:\n              cpu: \"1000m\"\n              memory: \"512Mi\"\n          readinessProbe:\n            httpGet:\n              path: /health\n              port: http\n            initialDelaySeconds: 10\n            periodSeconds: 5\n          livenessProbe:\n            httpGet:\n              path: /health\n              port: http\n            initialDelaySeconds: 30\n            periodSeconds: 10\n`\n\nThe Deployment declares three desired replicas. Its controller creates and maintains the Pods. Resource requests help the scheduler select suitable nodes, while limits constrain container consumption. Readiness determines whether a Pod should receive Service traffic. The example must be adapted to the actual application health endpoints and resource profile.\n\n## 6. Production DevOps Scenario\n\nConsider a Java payment application deployed to a Kubernetes cluster. The development team stores its source code and Kubernetes manifests in version control. A CI pipeline builds and tests the application, scans the image, and publishes an immutable image digest to a registry.\n\nA delivery process updates the Deployment manifest to reference the approved image. Kubernetes accepts the updated desired state and begins a rolling update. The scheduler selects nodes with sufficient available resources. Kubelets start the containers, and readiness probes determine when the new Pods can receive traffic.\n\nThe Service routes requests to ready endpoints. Monitoring records application latency, error rates, Pod restarts, CPU usage, memory usage, and node health. If the new version fails readiness or application validation, the delivery process stops or rolls back according to the deployment policy.\n\nThe team records the source commit, image digest, manifest revision, deployment time, and validation result. This makes it possible to identify exactly which application version is running during an incident.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes and Their Impact\n\n- Applying manifests to the wrong Kubernetes context can change an unintended cluster.\n- Incorrect labels or selectors can leave Services without matching Pod endpoints.\n- Missing resource requests can make scheduling unpredictable under resource pressure.\n- Excessively low memory limits can cause containers to be terminated with OOMKilled.\n- Incorrect readiness probes can prevent healthy applications from receiving traffic.\n- Incorrect liveness probes can repeatedly restart an application that is still recovering.\n- Using mutable image tags weakens deployment traceability.\n- Broad RBAC permissions increase the impact of compromised identities.\n- Deleting PersistentVolumeClaims without understanding reclaim behavior can cause data loss.\n- Assuming Pod creation means application readiness can cause outages.\n- Ignoring namespace boundaries can lead to incorrect resource inspection or modification.\n\n## 8. Troubleshooting and Safe Recovery\n\nStart by confirming the active context, namespace, resource name, and timestamp of the failure. Inspect resource state using kubectl get and kubectl describe. Review events for scheduling failures, image-pull errors, mount failures, probe failures, and node pressure.\n\nFor a Pending Pod, inspect scheduling events, node capacity, resource requests, taints, tolerations, and affinity. For ImagePullBackOff, verify the image reference, registry availability, and image-pull credentials. For CrashLoopBackOff, inspect current and previous container logs, exit codes, commands, configuration, and resource limits.\n\nFor service connectivity problems, inspect Service selectors, EndpointSlices, DNS, network policies, and the cluster network implementation. For storage failures, inspect PVC binding, PV state, CSI components, access modes, and mount events.\n\nApply the smallest safe correction. Preserve evidence before deleting Pods or changing shared resources. Verify the recovery through resource conditions and an actual application request.\n\n## 9. Security and Production Best Practices\n\nUse least-privilege RBAC for human users and service accounts. Avoid granting cluster-admin access to application workloads. Protect Secrets through encryption at rest, restrictive access, controlled rotation, and secure external secret integration where required.\n\nUse trusted and scanned container images. Run containers as non-root where possible, drop unnecessary Linux capabilities, restrict privilege escalation, and use appropriate seccomp and Pod security controls. Apply network policies when supported by the cluster network plugin.\n\nSet realistic resource requests and limits. Configure readiness, liveness, and startup probes according to application behavior. Use controlled rollout strategies, topology-aware scheduling, disruption budgets, and capacity planning for important services.\n\nBack up stateful data and test restoration. Monitor control-plane access, node health, Pod restarts, application SLOs, storage capacity, and cluster resource pressure. Keep cluster versions, add-ons, and workload manifests within supported compatibility ranges.\n\n## 10. Verification and Completion Criteria\n\nThe operation represented by **Test Annotations — Testing Strategy and Fundamentals** is complete only when the declared and actual cluster states are consistent and the application behavior has been verified.\n\nCheck that:\n\n- The intended cluster context and namespace were used.\n- The correct API resources were created or updated.\n- Selectors and labels connect the intended resources.\n- Pods are scheduled and containers start successfully.\n- Readiness and health checks report the expected state.\n- Services expose the intended endpoints.\n- Required storage is mounted and persistent data is safe.\n- Resource requests, limits, and security settings match policy.\n- Events and logs contain no unresolved operational failures.\n- The application responds correctly through its intended access path.\n- The image and manifest revisions can be traced to the approved release.\n\n## 11. Practical Exercise\n\nCreate a namespace and deploy a small application using a Deployment manifest. Add a Service that selects the application Pods. Inspect the Deployment, ReplicaSet, Pods, Service, and endpoints.\n\nChange the image reference to an invalid image and observe the resulting image-pull failure. Use kubectl describe and events to identify the reason. Restore the valid image reference and observe the successful rollout.\n\nTest the application through the Service. Explain the difference between a Pod being created, a container running, a Pod becoming ready, and the application successfully responding to a request.\n\n## 12. Interview and Engineering Understanding\n\nAn engineer should be able to explain what **Test Annotations — Testing Strategy and Fundamentals** means, why Kubernetes provides the relevant capability, which API resources and cluster components participate, how reconciliation or execution works, and how the final state is verified.\n\nA strong practical answer describes the desired state, the actual state before and after the operation, the relevant manifest fields or commands, the evidence collected, and the production risks. Avoid simply stating that kubectl apply was successful. Explain whether the workload was scheduled, started, became ready, and served real application traffic."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-5-topic-3-section-2",
        "title": "Test Annotations — Framework Architecture",
        "content": "# Test Annotations — Framework Architecture\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThis section belongs to **Test Annotations** in Stage 05: Kubernetes Container Orchestration.\n\nKubernetes manages containerized applications through a declarative API and a set of controllers that continuously reconcile actual cluster state with the desired state declared in resource specifications. The relevant components may include Pods, workload controllers, Services, networking, storage, configuration resources, scheduling, security controls, and observability systems. The engineering objective is to run applications reliably, securely, and predictably across a cluster.\n\nExplain the architecture and responsibilities of the components involved. Describe API requests, authentication and authorization, admission processing, persistence in etcd, controller reconciliation, scheduling, kubelet coordination, and container-runtime interaction where relevant. Identify which component makes the decision, which component executes the work, and how the resulting state becomes visible through the Kubernetes API. Explain the impact of control-plane or node-component failures.\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Kubernetes resources and components involved, perform the relevant operation, inspect cluster state, and recognize the risks of an incorrect implementation.\n\n## 2. Kubernetes Technical Architecture and Behavior\n\nKubernetes uses a declarative operating model. An engineer declares the desired state in API resources, and Kubernetes controllers continuously work to reconcile the actual cluster state with that desired state.\n\nThe API server is the main entry point for cluster operations. Authentication, authorization, and admission controls determine whether a request can be accepted. etcd stores cluster state. Controllers monitor resources and create or update dependent objects. The scheduler assigns unscheduled Pods to suitable nodes. The kubelet on each worker node works with the container runtime to ensure assigned workloads run as specified.\n\nFor **Test Annotations — Framework Architecture**, identify the API resource, controller, node component, or external integration responsible for the behavior. Explain what state is submitted, what reconciliation takes place, and how the result can be observed. Do not assume that a successful kubectl apply means the application is healthy. API acceptance, scheduling, container startup, readiness, and application availability are separate conditions.\n\n## 3. Implementation Workflow\n\n1. Confirm the current Kubernetes context with kubectl config current-context.\n2. Select and verify the correct namespace.\n3. Inspect the relevant resources before changing anything.\n4. Review the manifest fields, image reference, labels, selectors, resources, and permissions.\n5. Apply the required configuration using a reviewed manifest or approved deployment process.\n6. Observe controller reconciliation and Pod scheduling.\n7. Inspect Pod conditions, container state, events, and logs.\n8. Verify networking, storage, and application readiness where required.\n9. Record the manifest revision, image digest, and resulting workload state.\n\nUse namespaces and explicit resource names to avoid changing the wrong environment. Before deleting or replacing resources, determine whether they own persistent data or production traffic.\n\n## 4. Practical Kubernetes Commands\n\nThe following commands provide relevant inspection and operational examples. Replace placeholder resource names and namespaces with actual values.\n\n`\bash id=\"k8s501\"\nkubectl cluster-info\nkubectl get nodes -o wide\nkubectl get namespaces\nkubectl get pods -A\nkubectl get services -A\nkubectl get events -A --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp\n`\n\nCommands that modify resources should be executed only after checking the active context and reviewing the target manifest. Avoid copying production credentials or secret values into terminal history.\n\n## 5. Kubernetes Workload Example\n\nThe following Deployment demonstrates a basic stateless application workload:\n\n`yaml id=\"k8s502\"\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: payments-api\n  namespace: production\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: payments-api\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: payments-api\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: application\n          image: registry.example.com/payments-api:1.4.0\n          ports:\n            - name: http\n              containerPort: 8080\n          resources:\n            requests:\n              cpu: \"250m\"\n              memory: \"256Mi\"\n            limits:\n              cpu: \"1000m\"\n              memory: \"512Mi\"\n          readinessProbe:\n            httpGet:\n              path: /health\n              port: http\n            initialDelaySeconds: 10\n            periodSeconds: 5\n          livenessProbe:\n            httpGet:\n              path: /health\n              port: http\n            initialDelaySeconds: 30\n            periodSeconds: 10\n`\n\nThe Deployment declares three desired replicas. Its controller creates and maintains the Pods. Resource requests help the scheduler select suitable nodes, while limits constrain container consumption. Readiness determines whether a Pod should receive Service traffic. The example must be adapted to the actual application health endpoints and resource profile.\n\n## 6. Production DevOps Scenario\n\nConsider a Java payment application deployed to a Kubernetes cluster. The development team stores its source code and Kubernetes manifests in version control. A CI pipeline builds and tests the application, scans the image, and publishes an immutable image digest to a registry.\n\nA delivery process updates the Deployment manifest to reference the approved image. Kubernetes accepts the updated desired state and begins a rolling update. The scheduler selects nodes with sufficient available resources. Kubelets start the containers, and readiness probes determine when the new Pods can receive traffic.\n\nThe Service routes requests to ready endpoints. Monitoring records application latency, error rates, Pod restarts, CPU usage, memory usage, and node health. If the new version fails readiness or application validation, the delivery process stops or rolls back according to the deployment policy.\n\nThe team records the source commit, image digest, manifest revision, deployment time, and validation result. This makes it possible to identify exactly which application version is running during an incident.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes and Their Impact\n\n- Applying manifests to the wrong Kubernetes context can change an unintended cluster.\n- Incorrect labels or selectors can leave Services without matching Pod endpoints.\n- Missing resource requests can make scheduling unpredictable under resource pressure.\n- Excessively low memory limits can cause containers to be terminated with OOMKilled.\n- Incorrect readiness probes can prevent healthy applications from receiving traffic.\n- Incorrect liveness probes can repeatedly restart an application that is still recovering.\n- Using mutable image tags weakens deployment traceability.\n- Broad RBAC permissions increase the impact of compromised identities.\n- Deleting PersistentVolumeClaims without understanding reclaim behavior can cause data loss.\n- Assuming Pod creation means application readiness can cause outages.\n- Ignoring namespace boundaries can lead to incorrect resource inspection or modification.\n\n## 8. Troubleshooting and Safe Recovery\n\nStart by confirming the active context, namespace, resource name, and timestamp of the failure. Inspect resource state using kubectl get and kubectl describe. Review events for scheduling failures, image-pull errors, mount failures, probe failures, and node pressure.\n\nFor a Pending Pod, inspect scheduling events, node capacity, resource requests, taints, tolerations, and affinity. For ImagePullBackOff, verify the image reference, registry availability, and image-pull credentials. For CrashLoopBackOff, inspect current and previous container logs, exit codes, commands, configuration, and resource limits.\n\nFor service connectivity problems, inspect Service selectors, EndpointSlices, DNS, network policies, and the cluster network implementation. For storage failures, inspect PVC binding, PV state, CSI components, access modes, and mount events.\n\nApply the smallest safe correction. Preserve evidence before deleting Pods or changing shared resources. Verify the recovery through resource conditions and an actual application request.\n\n## 9. Security and Production Best Practices\n\nUse least-privilege RBAC for human users and service accounts. Avoid granting cluster-admin access to application workloads. Protect Secrets through encryption at rest, restrictive access, controlled rotation, and secure external secret integration where required.\n\nUse trusted and scanned container images. Run containers as non-root where possible, drop unnecessary Linux capabilities, restrict privilege escalation, and use appropriate seccomp and Pod security controls. Apply network policies when supported by the cluster network plugin.\n\nSet realistic resource requests and limits. Configure readiness, liveness, and startup probes according to application behavior. Use controlled rollout strategies, topology-aware scheduling, disruption budgets, and capacity planning for important services.\n\nBack up stateful data and test restoration. Monitor control-plane access, node health, Pod restarts, application SLOs, storage capacity, and cluster resource pressure. Keep cluster versions, add-ons, and workload manifests within supported compatibility ranges.\n\n## 10. Verification and Completion Criteria\n\nThe operation represented by **Test Annotations — Framework Architecture** is complete only when the declared and actual cluster states are consistent and the application behavior has been verified.\n\nCheck that:\n\n- The intended cluster context and namespace were used.\n- The correct API resources were created or updated.\n- Selectors and labels connect the intended resources.\n- Pods are scheduled and containers start successfully.\n- Readiness and health checks report the expected state.\n- Services expose the intended endpoints.\n- Required storage is mounted and persistent data is safe.\n- Resource requests, limits, and security settings match policy.\n- Events and logs contain no unresolved operational failures.\n- The application responds correctly through its intended access path.\n- The image and manifest revisions can be traced to the approved release.\n\n## 11. Practical Exercise\n\nCreate a namespace and deploy a small application using a Deployment manifest. Add a Service that selects the application Pods. Inspect the Deployment, ReplicaSet, Pods, Service, and endpoints.\n\nChange the image reference to an invalid image and observe the resulting image-pull failure. Use kubectl describe and events to identify the reason. Restore the valid image reference and observe the successful rollout.\n\nTest the application through the Service. Explain the difference between a Pod being created, a container running, a Pod becoming ready, and the application successfully responding to a request.\n\n## 12. Interview and Engineering Understanding\n\nAn engineer should be able to explain what **Test Annotations — Framework Architecture** means, why Kubernetes provides the relevant capability, which API resources and cluster components participate, how reconciliation or execution works, and how the final state is verified.\n\nA strong practical answer describes the desired state, the actual state before and after the operation, the relevant manifest fields or commands, the evidence collected